<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>1.6. Finding potentials</title>

  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-conservative-implies-curl">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>\mbf F</m> is a conservative vector field (i.e. if <m>\mbf F = \nabla f</m> for some 
        <m>f</m>), then <md>
          \nabla \times \mbf F = \nabla \times (\nabla f) = \zerovec
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <alert>However, the reverse is not always true without an additional condition!</alert>
  
  <theorem xml:id="thm-curl-implies-conservative">
    <statement>  
      <p>
        If <m>\nabla \times \mbf F = \zerovec</m> in a simply connected domain <m>D</m>, then 
        <m>\mbf F</m> is a conservative field within <m>D</m> and we can find a potential function in <m>D</m> such that <m>
          \mbf F = \nabla f
        </m> within the domain <m>D</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

      <p>
        To find the <term>potential function <m>f</m></term>, choose an arbitrary basepoint <m>*</m> 
        and integrate over any path from that basepoint to some point 
        <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>. Then the potential function is <md>f(x_0,y_0)= \work{C}{F}</md>.
      </p>
  

  <exercise workspace="0in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Let's consider the field <m>\mbf F(x,y) = (2xy,x^2)</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify that
  <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are two paths with the same initial and
  final points, explain why <m>\ds \work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's find a potential function for <m>F</m> by choosing the basepoint <m>*
  = (0,0)</m>.  For a point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>, integrate over this curve <m>C</m>
  to define
  <md>
  \ds f(x_0,y_0) = \work{C}{F}.
  </md></p>
       <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
      <shortdescription>The curve in which we integrate over, see description.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic1">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="0">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,4,4)">
            <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" />
            <path start="(0,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
              <lineto point="(3,0)"/>
              <lineto point="(3,2)"/>
            </path>
            <point p="(0,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"></point>
            <point p="(3,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
            <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment = "nw" offset="(-5,3)">
              <m>* </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(3,2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
              <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description> 
        <p> The path begins at the origin, travels along the x-axis until some value <m>x_0</m>, 
      then travels vertically until it reaches the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>Verify that the function <m>f(x,y)</m> that you have found is a potential
  for <m>\mbf F</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the field
  <md> 
    \ds \mbf F(x,y) = \left(-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}, \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\right).
  </md>
  We have seen that <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m> but that the field is not
  defined at the origin <m>(0,0)</m> so that the domain of the field is not
  simply connected.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's attempt to find a potential function by integrating.  Choose the
  basepoint to be <m>(1,0)</m>.  To define <m>\ds f(x_0, y_0)</m>, integrate along
  the positive <m>x</m>-axis and then along a circular arc traversed in the
  counterclockwise direction. (Polar coordinates could be useful for part of this...) 
  </p>
        <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>The curve in which we integrate over to find a potential function, see description.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic2">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,7,7)">
            <axes  decorations="no" />
            <path start="(1,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
              <lineto point="(6,0)"/>
              <arc center="(0,0)" radius="6" range="(0,60)"/>
            </path>
            <point p="(1,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"></point>
            <point p="(6*cos(pi/3),6*sin(pi/3))" stroke="blue" fill="blue"></point>

            <label anchor="(1,0)" alignment = "s" offset="(25,-3)">
              <m>* = (1,0) </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
              <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description> 
        <p>The path begins at the a basepoint <m>(1,0)</m>, travels right along the <m>x</m>-axis for some time, 
      then travels along a circular arc centered at the origin until it reaches the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What do you
  find for <m>f(2,0)</m>?  <m>f(0,2)</m>?  <m>f(-2,0)</m>?  <m>f(0,-2)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is a general expression for <m>f(x_0, y_0)</m>?  It may be
  helpful to think in terms of polar coordinates.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Why doesn't <m>f</m> define a potential function for <m>\mbf F</m> in the region
  where <m>\mbf F</m> is defined?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Hmmm... Let's try to avoid the problem (the origin) by literally going around the problem.  If <m>C</m> is the curve shown below, what is <m>\work{C}{F}</m>?</p>
        <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>The closed curve in which we integrate over to find the work, see description.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic3">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
            <axes  decorations="no" />
            <path start="(6,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
              <arc center="(0,0)" radius="6" range="(0,345)"/>
              <lineto point="(2*cos(345),2*sin(345))"/>
            </path>
            <path start="(2*cos(345),2*sin(345))" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="3">
              <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" range="(345,0)"/>
              <lineto point="(6,0)"/>
            </path>
            <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
              <m>C</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description> 
        <p>The path begins at a point on the positive <m>x</m>-axis, travels right along the <m>x</m>-axis for some time, 
      then travels counterclockwise along a circular arc centered at the origin until it almost goes full circle, 
      then it cuts left until just to the left and below the initial starting point on the positive <m>x</m>-axis,
      then travels clockwise along a circular arc centered at the origin until it connects back with the initial point.
      </p>
      </description>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now imagine the two radial pieces of <m>C</m> merging together and explain
  why <m>\ds \work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m> where <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are the
  two circles below.</p>
    <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
      <shortdescription>Two co-centric circles around the origin traveling counterclockwise.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic4">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
            <axes  decorations="no" />
            <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" range="(40,400)" arrows="1"
                stroke="blue" thickness="3" reverse="no" arrow-location="3"/>
            <arc center="(0,0)" radius="6" range="(40,405)" arrows="1"
                stroke="blue" thickness="3" reverse="no"/>
            <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-3,3)">
              <m>C_1</m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(2,2)" offset="(5,0)">
              <m>C_2</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description> 
        <p>
          Two circles are centered at the origin and oriented counterclockwise, 
          with <m>C_1</m> the circle with a smaller radius and <m>C_2</m> the larger radius.
      </p></description>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are the two curves shown below, explain why
  <m>\ds \work{C_1}{F} = \work{C_2}{F}</m>.</p>

  <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>Two "squiggly" co-centric curves around the origin traveling counterclockwise.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic5">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
            <axes  decorations="no" />
            <spline points="((2,0),(2,1),(1,2),(-1,2),(-2,0),(-1,-2),(1,-1),(2,0))"
            closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="2"/>
            <spline points="((5,0),(4,4),(1,4),(-1,5),(-4,0),(-1,-5),(1,-3),(5,0))"
            closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="1"/>
            <label anchor="(4,3)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,3)">
              <m>C_1</m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(3,1)" offset="(1,4)">
              <m>C_2</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>\ds \work{C}{F}</m> if <m>C</m> is the curve below.</p>
        <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>One squiggly curve traversing the origin counterclockwise three times before crossing itself to connect back to its initial point.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-7-pic6">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
            <axes  decorations="no" />
            <spline points="((1,1),(0.5,2),(-1,2),(-2,0),(-1,-2),(1,-2),(3,-1), (3,1),(1,4),(-2,3),(-4,0),(-2,-2),(0,-5),(3,-2),(5,0),(4,4),(1,6),(-3,5),(-6,0),(-3,-6),(1,-6),(4,-4),(6,0),(6,5),(4,6),(2.5,1),(1,1))"
            closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="22.6"/>
        
            <label anchor="(-5,4)" alignment="ne" offset="(-4,3)">
              <m>C</m>
            </label>
    
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
